{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — construct sweep (rebar-debug Step 7 Axis 1), confirmed root cause\n\n**Class (proven by 7dea/2329 completion_verdict_cache + d284/2334 sizing.save_checkpoint):** an atomic-write site composes a DETERMINISTIC temp name shared across writers (`tmp = f\"{self.path}.tmp\"`), writes it, then `os.replace(tmp, final)`. Two concurrent writers for the same final path both write the one shared temp; the first `os.replace` consumes it and the second raises `FileNotFoundError`, which the site swallows → a silently-lost write (and, for these fail-open stores, `_dirty` stays True and a temp may be left as residue).\n\n### Sweep results (each hit confirmed semantically ±10 lines + per-subsystem concurrency analysis)\n\n**CONFIRMED SIBLINGS (2) — live-collision-possible, fix in scope:**\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/peer_confirmations.py` — `PeerConfirmationStore.save()` L188-196: `tmp_path = f\"{self.path}.tmp\"` → `os.replace`; `except OSError` swallowed.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/impossible_links.py` — `ImpossibleLinkStore.save()` L372-380: identical construct.\n\nConcurrency verdict: these write the per-repo `.tickets-tracker/.bridge_state/{peer_confirmations,impossible_links}.json` (committed to the tickets branch). The reconciler serializes passes via the `refs/reconciler/lock` pass-lock, BUT (a) the lease is steal-able when expired and (b) there is **no `abort_check`/lock-lost guard immediately before the persistence-phase `save()`** (`reconcile._save_and_commit_bindings` / `_persist_and_log`) — only the apply loop is guarded. So in the lease-steal/zombie-pass window two passes can reach `save()` on the same sidecar concurrently → live collision. **Decisive design-invariant evidence:** the SAME package already standardized on unique-per-writer `tempfile.mkstemp` for its five OTHER atomic-write sites (`pass_io._write_mapping_json_atomic`, `binding_repository._write_json_atomically` — docstring: \"so an aborted pass leaves no litter beside the real state\", `apply_planning`, `get_rotation`, `last_pass`). These two sidecar writers are the only reconciler atomic-writes deviating — an omission, not a deliberate design.\n\n**EXCLUDED (analyzed, not live siblings):**\n- `review_bot/reconcile.py::_write_cursor` (L170-181) — single-writer-by-design: the cursor is written only from the single `reconcile_loop` asyncio task, which `await`s each `reconcile_once` sequentially (`while True: await reconcile_once` — passes never overlap); `_write_cursor` is synchronous; explicit \"single-box PoC\" (app.py:79). No concurrent same-cursor writers.\n- `_config_writer.py:281`, `_cli/__init__.py:254` — one-shot user config writes (`rebar init`/config); single-writer.\n- `_opcert_signing.py:80` — `tmp = f\"{pub_path}.{os.getpid()}.tmp\"` is PID-unique across processes; the `.pub` is a DETERMINISTIC pure function of the private key (`ssh-keygen -y`), so even under a same-process multi-thread race the \"losing\" write is byte-identical → no data loss; genesis is one-shot and the private key commit is race-safe via `os.link`. Not a meaningful sibling.\n- Safe (unique-per-writer / under write-lock, not siblings): `_store/fsutil.atomic_write` (canonical mkstemp), `_store/staging.py` (`.tmp-newticket-<pid>-<uuid4>` + store write-lock), `llm/overlap/queue.py` (mkstemp), `llm/workflow/prompt_authoring._atomic_write`→fsutil, `alert_store`→fsutil, plus the five reconciler mkstemp sites above and the two already-fixed files (2329/2334).\n\n### Defect attribution (omission-class; RED-first regression to follow)\n- `peer_confirmations.py` construct introduced by commit `c638bc6` (\"Record peer confirmation for pushed links\") → `rebar-ticket: 248f-ac00-18db-4259`.\n- `impossible_links.py` construct introduced by commit `54bdc0f` (\"Stop retrying impossible inbound links\") → `rebar-ticket: b8b1-6462-b4f1-4a1d`.\n\nBoth sites were created with the shared-`.tmp` construct (2026-08-12), PREDATING the package's mkstemp hardening (`binding_repository`, 2026-08-19, `4758d47`) — so this is an omission (the hardening never reached these two), not a regression. `caused_by` edges recorded to BOTH origins; on close I will pass `--caused-by=248f-ac00-18db-4259` (peer_confirmations, the primary/first-listed site) and the b8b1 edge remains via the explicit link. Divergence from a whole-file blame majority: attribution is per-site-of-introduction, not the fix site.\n\n### Fix plan (both siblings, one change — proven pattern, fail-open PRESERVED)\nReplace the shared `tmp_path = f\"{self.path}.tmp\"` with `fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=<dir>, prefix=<basename>., suffix=\".tmp\")`, write via `os.fdopen(fd)`, `os.replace(tmp, self.path)` onto the shared final (last-write-wins preserved); on `OSError` unlink ONLY the writer's own temp (`missing_ok`) then log-and-return (fail-open contract kept — these stores must never raise into the pass). Compact, stdlib-only (`import tempfile`), no module-size/complexity impact (files 346/383 LOC)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787950943895746001,"uuid":"4fd77888-28a2-4492-9b54-b29c3611a907"}